Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — scene draft
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-09-28
Status: SC010 proposed for individual author scene review; combined pass provisionally accepted

INT. SERVICE-STATION TOILETS/RESTAURANT - AFTERNOON

Zoe bolts the main toilet door. The sound catches her. She unbolts it, bolts it again, then checks the cubicles and narrow frosted window. Her watch counts down from three minutes. Kitchen workers pass outside with rubbish bags; ordinary complaints travel through the glass.

She looks at an unanswered message to JACKIE. Her hand hovers over the name. She locks her phone without calling. Her expression hardens, then falters. When the final second passes, Zoe unlocks the door herself.

She checks the corridor window. Back at the booth, Elias's half-eaten pie and cash remain; his chair is empty. Zoe sits in her original place, keeps her bag close, and watches the awning through the mirror. The WAITRESS brings the bill and asks if the man with her is returning.

ZOE
He isn't my dad. He said to pay. Keep the change.

The waitress asks if all is well. Zoe says yes while watching a man touch an earpiece. Another shifts to cover the car. A third near the portable toilets turns toward the service yard. Their positions tighten, then hesitate. Zoe has no view of Elias.

A chair moves opposite her. Elias is back, dusty, a thin line of blood on one knuckle. Zoe nearly reacts.

ELIAS
Don't. Walk on my left. Normally.

ZOE
You're bleeding.

ELIAS
Not mine.

INT./EXT. RESTAURANT/SERVICE-STATION CAR PARK - CONTINUOUS

Elias walks toward the doors at an ordinary pace. Zoe takes his left. A waiter with plates, a child with a drink and two lorry drivers interrupt their path. Neither rushes. Outside, two men keep their hands visible but watch Zoe like a target. A football rolls across her path; she steps over it.

From between the coaches comes a TALL MAN in a black jacket. Zoe recognises the authority he had at Cain's house from how others watch him. He sees Elias and loses colour. His hand moves away from his pocket.

TALL MAN
Elias.

Elias keeps walking. Zoe almost stops; he matches her shortened stride, then leads her around a parked van. The tall man calls again but does not pursue. A man near their car moves to narrow the approach.

Zoe's backpack catches on the rear door frame. She frees it, gets in and pulls the door shut. Elias sits behind the wheel. The central locks drop; Zoe tests the inside handle. It still works. The engine coughs twice before catching. An estate car blocks their reverse path for a pedestrian; Elias waits rather than forcing through. A pursuer touches the roof, then recoils after looking into Elias's face.

The lane clears. Elias reverses and drives with control. The men run toward a dark saloon and a black four-by-four. The saloon's tyres collapse under it; the four-by-four drops at one corner and stops at a divider. Zoe sees only the effect. The tall man remains behind with a phone to his ear. The hatchback reaches the slip road.

ZOE
He knew you. What did you do to those cars?

ELIAS
Delayed them. I'll explain what I can when we're clear.

His injured hand tightens on the wheel. Zoe looks back once, then at the unlocked handle.
